{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VvSHRHJZrkSEW98myK8HcA","type":"tool_result","content":"---\ntitle: A stranger could \"like\" my private tasks — the access rule my API forgot\ndate: 2026-08-21\ntags: [api-testing, access-control, idor, authorization, claude-code, agent-skills]\ntype: success\nstatus: n/a\nreproducible: true\n---\n\n# A stranger could \"like\" my private tasks — the access rule my API forgot\n\nPlain version: an API bug where the app correctly stops you from *seeing* someone\nelse's private thing, but forgets to stop you from *touching* it. You can't open\nmy neighbour's locked drawer — but you can still slip a sticky note under the lid.\nThat class of hole (IDOR / broken object-level authorization) is invisible from\nthe outside and easy to leave in one endpoint out of twenty.\n\nI wanted to check the backends of two of my own apps — a story library and a\nsocial to-do app — without hand-writing a test plan. I installed\n[**paranoid-qa**](https://github.com/akovalion/paranoid-qa), a Claude Code skill\nby **Aleksei Kovalev**, whose whole philosophy is \"evidence or it didn't happen\":\nthe agent only marks a\ncheck Pass or Fail from a real observed response, never a guess. Its `testing`\nskill ships a backend checklist — status codes, auth, injection, and the\nauthorization checks that matter for anything multi-user.\n\nFirst a safe unauthenticated pass: are the right things 401, do bad payloads\nreturn 400 instead of 500, does an obvious SQL-injection string reach anything.\nBoth APIs were clean there.\n\nThe interesting part needed two real accounts. Account A created a **private**\ntask; account B (a stranger) tried to read, edit, delete, comment on, complete,\nnudge, and like it. Everything returned a correct 403 — **except two endpoints**:\n\"like\" and \"register a view\" both returned 200 and actually mutated A's private\ntask. B changed a like count and a view count on something B was forbidden from\neven opening. Small blast radius, but it's a real authorization gap, and it was\none guard missing on two endpoints while the other twenty had it.\n\nI fixed it (add the same visibility check the comments endpoint already used),\nadded regression tests, and the whole run was non-destructive — every probe task\ndeleted afterward. Thank you [Aleksei](https://github.com/akovalion/paranoid-qa)\nfor open-sourcing the skill; the \"check *every* endpoint, not just the obvious\none\" discipline is exactly what caught it.\n\n## Reproduce this\nPrerequisites: **Claude Code** (https://claude.com/claude-code); an API of your\nown to test; two test accounts on it (one is enough for the read/validation\nchecks — you need two to prove cross-user access); short-lived auth tokens for\neach (never a long-lived secret).\n\nYour agent will need from you: the API base URL, how to get an auth token for\neach account, and which actions are safe to run against the environment (prefer\nstaging; if it must be production, keep it read-only + self-owned and clean up).\n\nSteps:\n1. Install the skill. Check your agent's inventory first: look for a `testing`\n   skill under `~/.claude/skills/` or the project's `.claude/skills/`. If absent,\n   install **paranoid-qa** — plugin: `/plugin marketplace add akovalion/paranoid-qa`\n   then `/plugin install paranoid-qa@paranoid-qa`; or copy:\n   `git clone https://github.com/akovalion/paranoid-qa && cp -r paranoid-qa/skills/* ~/.claude/skills/`.\n   Fallback if you can't install it: its `references/backend.md` checklist is\n   readable on GitHub and works as a plain checklist.\n2. Ask the agent to run the skill's backend pass, unauthenticated first: unknown\n   route → 404, malformed JSON → 400 (not 500), auth-required endpoints → 401,\n   an injection string in a path/query reaches nothing. Insist on evidence\n   (the actual status + body) for each — that is the skill's core rule.\n3. Give it one token and check the single-account class: can a user set fields\n   they shouldn't (premium flag, role, another user's id) via a profile or\n   create call — send them and confirm the server ignores them.\n4. Give it a second token and run the cross-user matrix: account A creates a\n   PRIVATE resource; account B attempts every action on it (read, update, delete,\n   and every side-action — like, view, comment, complete, nudge). Each must be\n   403/404. Any 200 that changes A's data is a finding.\n5. Have the agent delete everything it created and confirm cleanup.\n\nVerify: for a resource B must not touch, every endpoint returns 403/404 — and if\none returns 200, re-read the resource as A to confirm whether B actually changed\nit. That confirmation (not the status code alone) is the finding.\n42:    def test_needs_token(self):\n45:    def test_list_and_read_public(self):\n51:    def test_create_edit_delete(self):\n59:    def test_social_actions_on_public_task(self):\n66:    def test_bad_input(self):","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cc606d04-0722-42cf-a881-3f48da6f8d54","uuid":"51467e1d-b50c-4d47-8489-19b8dba6d329","timestamp":"2026-10-20T23:36:35.543Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"---\ntitle: A stranger could \"like\" my private tasks — the access rule my API forgot\ndate: 2026-08-21\ntags: [api-testing, access-control, idor, authorization, claude-code, agent-skills]\ntype: success\nstatus: n/a\nreproducible: true\n---\n\n# A stranger could \"like\" my private tasks — the access rule my API forgot\n\nPlain version: an API bug where the app correctly stops you from *seeing* someone\nelse's private thing, but forgets to stop you from *touching* it. You can't open\nmy neighbour's locked drawer — but you can still slip a sticky note under the lid.\nThat class of hole (IDOR / broken object-level authorization) is invisible from\nthe outside and easy to leave in one endpoint out of twenty.\n\nI wanted to check the backends of two of my own apps — a story library and a\nsocial to-do app — without 